From b35525ae216a7f5f0767c503bf3ab870eda1947d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 19:42:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoShaders][GLES] The 1D LUT of the display in
 Kodi's own video shaders

The SDR pictures that Kodi draws itself take the display's LUT in the
shader that writes them to the window (gles_lut3d.frag). A LUT with a
1D LUT (a .cube of LUT_1D_SIZE alone or as Resolve's shaper, ArgyllCMS's
.cal) now has it there too, as the bridge applies it:
gles_lut3d_curve.frag, put in before gles_lut3d.frag only where the LUT
has one, reads its points from a 2D texture of 32-bit floats of their
own (rows of 4096, read at the centres of the texels, in highp) and
interpolates each channel linearly; gles_lut3d.frag calls it after the
input range, before the grid. Without a 1D LUT the shader is the one of
a 3D LUT alone (the call is behind a define that only that file sets).

CLut3dGLES::PutCurve() puts the curves on the GPU with the table, at
once and straight from the LUT (768 KB at most), on unit 6; Bind()
binds them where the program has their uniform. The shaders are made
again where a 1D LUT comes or goes, also where one LUT takes another's
place within a frame, and kodi.log says the size of the 1D LUT with the
table.
